  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe cold front is progressing through the region from south to \nnorth. Recent SFC analysis puts the front just south of I-80. \nRain showers and stratus ahead of the front have muddled up the \nwarm sector quite a bit for any convection across the heart of \nthe region. Temperatures are stuck in the low 70s across much of\nthe region and the heating needed to produce instability hasn't\ndeveloped. With better forcing along the front, updrafts have \nbeen trying to fire but have been stymied by the ample dry air \nabove 600mb. This dry air starting near 0C likely precludes \nstrong updrafts and even lightning production. Additional lift \nprovided by convergence along the eastern ridges could allow for\na little more organization and the Marginal Risk from SPC has \nbeen pushed eastward to account for this. \n\nLoss of diurnal heating and waning upper support results in \ndwindling rain and storm activity after 21z tonight, with all \nrisks tapering off by 00z.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe upper pattern features a closed upper trough meandering over\nthe New England region late tonight through Thursday. Weak\nshortwave movement around this low plus wrap-around moisture\ncould foster isolated to scattered diurnal storms Wednesday\n(east of I-77) and Thursday (mainly along higher terrain) in\nwhat otherwise would be a drying period with slowly building\nhigh pressure.\n\nThese storms are unlikely to pose risks due to limited\ninstability (less than 500 J/kg).",
